  "verdict": "growth_wired_default_off",
  "training_needed_for_effect": "unanswered",
  "plain_language": {
    "summary": "This run installs a memory-capacity growth step at the boundary between sessions and checks three things in order. First, with growth switched off, every answer the model gives is byte-for-byte the same as before (same 154 questions, same 22 written faces, same parameter count and same identity fingerprint). Second, a session fills the memory to 28 of 32 places, the boundary doubles it to 64, and the next session keeps writing: nothing is dropped, nothing is overwritten, and everything written in the first session is still readable. Third, on the 22 correction faces the two-rule gate (is there room? is this write a copy of something already stored?) is compared with the arm that never grows; the count of faces that land on their own record is reported for both.",
    "can_prove": "if all three steps pass: on this model, growth can be attached to the real memory without changing any existing answer while switched off, and it can double the memory at a session boundary without losing a single stored record. That is a wiring and capacity result, and it is directly checkable.",
    "cannot_prove": "it cannot show that switching growth on makes the model better: the training question -- whether a grown memory needs training to become useful -- is not answered here. It also cannot show that the copy rule is safe to leave on: with today's averaged address the 22 faces already sit almost on top of each other, so a copy test may refuse writes that a better address would admit. Any such difference is reported per face, not smoothed over."
  },
